+# lab/bio3-2026-11: a third set of mechanisms taken from biology
+
+From a3cf1ba. Host: the WSL2 guest of the Ryzen host (12 threads, Go 1.24.7).
+Each arm's rule is committed before its reading. The arms of lab/bio-2026-11
+(B1 scent marks, B3 a fovea for hot entries) and lab/bio2-2026-11 (B4 spike
+timing, B5 larval slots; the postulates B6 a lek for hot entries and B7
+growth by segments) are not repeated.
+
+## The structure as an ecosystem: parts not yet matched
+
+lab/bio2's table matched the parts to organisms one by one. This one reads
+the table as a habitat that keys compete for, and adds the parts the
+earlier table left out.
+
+| Part | What it does | Its counterpart in nature | Where the counterpart does better |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| primary line, then alternate line | a key's two sites; a read tries the primary first, so a key in its alternate costs a second line | good habitat and marginal habitat | the ideal despotic distribution: dominants hold the good sites and subordinates are pushed to the margin. The filter's search pushes out whoever sits first in slot order (B8) |
+| the cuckoo search's choice of whom to move | first movable entry, slot 0 first | a vacancy chain started by whoever is nearest | territorial contests are settled by resource-holding power, not position (B8) |
+| a hot entry: a 64 B line in a side table, 1 per 128 slots, first come first served | wide counters for a heavy key, somewhere else | a nurse organ apart from the body (a placenta, a honey stomach shared by the hive) | the cuckoo chick: it grows where it hatched and pushes a nest-mate out; the nest is the resource, not a second site (B9) |
+| a register that overflows when every hot entry is taken (`HotRefused`) | the heavy key counts low, flagged | a territory system with more claimants than territories: floaters | where the resource is the nest itself there are no floaters: every chick has a nest-mate to push (B9) |
+| the reclaimer: 4 lines a call, 1/64 of touches, paced by the clock | frees expired slots; answers never depend on it | grooming while awake | hibernators rouse on a timer to do the upkeep that can't wait (interbout arousals): a deadline, not a pace (B10, postulate) |
+| a snapshot's clock continues the old timeline toward `MaxTick` | a lineage of snapshots ages | the soma ages | the germ line resets its clock: the offspring starts at zero (B11, postulate) |
+| the 4-bit epoch beside a 46-bit last touch | says which unit word1 is aligned to between a touch's two CASes | a checkpoint between two steps of the cell cycle | — (redundant at rest, needed in motion) |
+| `fp = 0` never stored (a key hashing to it gets 1) | a free word0 is 0 | a reserved codon (stop) | the code has three stop codons and uses them as punctuation: a word0 with `fp = 0` and other bits set matches no lookup, so it can mark a slot that is not an entry (B9 uses it) |
+
+## B8 Despotic placement
+
+**The analogy.** Where habitat differs in quality, animals do not spread
+evenly (the ideal free distribution); the dominant ones take the good sites
+and push the others to the margin (the ideal despotic distribution,
+Fretwell 1972). A key's primary line is the good site: a read finds it
+with one line. Its alternate line is the margin: a read loads the primary,
+misses, and loads the alternate, a second DRAM miss on a table past the
+cache. Who lives at the margin today is decided by slot order: an insert
+that finds both lines full moves the first entry it can (slot 0 of the
+primary line first), whatever its history. 18.9% of keys sit in their
+alternate line [D11], and a key touched a thousand times is as likely to
+be one of them as a key touched once.
+
+**The mechanism** (`TF_DESPOT=1`, in `claimAt`). The search tries its
+candidates in order of what the move does for reads, instead of slot
+order. The candidates are the eight entries of the two full lines, already
+loaded; the score reads only their words:
+
+- an entry in its alternate line (displaced bit set) would go *home* by the
+  move: the heaviest first;
+- then entries at home, which the move would send to the margin: the
+  lightest first (fewest events in the horizon, `mass`, as the eviction
+  scores them), oldest on ties.
+
+The same order is used at each depth of the iterative deepening for the
+first step of a path; deeper steps keep slot order. Nothing else changes:
+the same lines are warmed, the same budget is spent, the hand-off and move
+counts are untouched. Homing (E6) moved entries home with extra moves on
+inserts and hits; this adds no move, it chooses among the moves a search
+already makes.
+
+**Readings** (`TestLabDespot`, `TF_LAB=1`; the real filter, deterministic
+counts). Two streams at Capacity 200,000, 60 s horizon, 1 ms ticks:
+
+- *churn*: tfbench's stream (`workload.Churn`): a repeat is a key drawn
+  uniformly from those born in the last 1.5 horizons, so a key's past says
+  nothing of its future;
+- *80/20*: the same arrivals, but a fifth of the keys are marked popular
+  at birth and a repeat is one of those with probability 0.8.
+
+Read per arm: the share of hits (touches of a key the table holds) that
+find it in its alternate line; the share of live keys in their alternate
+line at the end; searches, the lines a search scans (budget spent),
+relocations, evictions; a digest of every answer (`Seen`, `Count`).
+
+**Rule (before any reading).** Worth timing if:
+
+1. on the 80/20 stream at load 1.00, hits found in the alternate line fall
+   to at most half the base's share;
+2. on the churn stream at load 1.00, that share is not above the base's
+   (at most 1.02 of it);
+3. at load 1.00 on both streams the answers' digest equals the base's (no
+   eviction there, so placement must not move an answer), and at load 1.15
+   evictions, relocations and lines scanned per search are each at most
+   1.10 of the base's;
+4. no key missed where the base misses none.
+
+If 1–4 hold, a timing reading follows (3 interleaved rounds in one binary,
+indicative only on this host): worth building if InsertSteady and
+MissInsert read at most 1.05 of the base.
+
+## B9 The cuckoo chick
+
+**The analogy.** A hot entry is an organ kept apart from the body: a
+heavy key's counters live in a side table, one 64 B entry per 128 slots,
+given to whoever asks first and refused when all are taken. lab/bio2's B6
+proposed a contest for them. The bird the table is named after does
+neither. A cuckoo chick needs more than its share of the nest, and it
+takes it where it is: it pushes a nest-mate over the rim. The nest-mate is
+the resource.
+
+**The mechanism.** A key whose register would overflow takes a second
+slot *of its own line* as its extension: a free slot, else the lightest
+line-mate that is a plain entry, which leaves as a search's step would
+move it (to its alternate line, by a path if need be) or, failing that, as
+an eviction would (to the stash). The extension holds the high parts of
+the seven counters. Its word0 has `fp = 0` with other bits set, which no
+key's lookup matches (a key whose hash gives 0 stores 1) and no free slot
+reads (a free word0 is 0), so lookups and the false-positive rate are
+unchanged; that leaves about 100 bits, 14 more per counter (bucket 0 to
+524,287 a unit). A hot key's touch stays in one cache line. A pair is
+pinned: searches don't move it; it is given up as a hot entry is (the
+rate falls, or the key expires). There is no hot table, and no refusal
+while a line has a plain mate: a line holds 4 slots, so a key with a pair
+beside it (3 slots) always has a fourth that is plain or free.
+
+**The model** (`TestLabChick`, `TF_LAB=1`). A placement model as lab/bio2's
+(`TestLabLarva`): the filter's lines, primary and alternate lines, its
+search as written there (iterative deepening to depth 3, the
+pressure-scaled budget), eviction of the lightest. Promotion at a 32nd
+event in one unit; release when a unit ends with 15 events or fewer in it,
+or at expiry (both arms alike). Two arms:
+
+- *table*: a hot table of slots/128 entries, first come first served;
+- *chick*: the mechanism above.
+
+Streams (Capacity 200,000, 60 s horizon, 1 ms ticks, 4 horizons):
+tfbench's churn at loads 1.00 and 1.15, with
+
+- S0: nothing added (validity);
+- S1: 1,000 heavy keys at a time (0.5% of Capacity), each at 20–200
+  events/s (log-uniform), living 1–2 horizons and replaced as they end;
+- S2: S1 after squatters: twice the hot table's entries in keys at
+  14 events/s each, from the start.
+
+Read: the share of the heavy keys' events that arrive while their key has
+wide counters or its unit holds 31 or fewer (*counted whole*); promotions,
+how each got its second slot (free, mate moved, mate evicted, refused);
+evictions and relocations, by who was evicted.
+
+**Rule (before any reading).** Worth building (a model's verdict) if:
+
+0. validity: on S0 the model's evictions are 0 at load 1.00 and within 15%
+   of the filter's 4,577 at 1.15, both arms; else void;
+1. on S2 the chick arm counts at least 0.99 of the heavy keys' events
+   whole. Void if the table arm counts more than 0.50 there (the scenario
+   shows no problem);
+2. on S1 at load 1.00 the chick arm evicts nothing (as the table arm),
+   and of the promotions that must move a mate at most 3% evict it;
+3. on S1 at load 1.15 its evictions are at most 1.10 of the table arm's;
+4. on S2 at load 1.00 no key that is not a squatter is evicted, and no
+   promotion is refused.
+
+Not read: the slot protocol for a pair (two slots changing hands, the
+touch's CASes on low and high parts), the sweep and the stash of a pair,
+the counters' real widths, speed.
+
+## B8b Earned territory
+
+Designed after B8's reading (clause 1 failed: hits found in the alternate
+line fell 0.1988 → 0.1827 on the 80/20 stream, not by half), so this rule
+is written knowing it. What the reading showed: 32–38% of live keys sit in
+their alternate line at load 1.00, but only 14–23% of first touches run a
+search; most displaced keys were *placed* in their alternate line, at
+their first touch, because their primary line was full and the other had
+room. A key is cold when it settles. The despot's choice at a search comes
+too early: a territory is contested once the claimant has shown what it
+is, not when it hatches.
+
+**The mechanism** (`TF_DESPOT` as bits: 1 B8's order, 2 homing, 4 the
+challenge). A touch that finds its key in its alternate line, and leaves
+it with 2, 4, 8, ... events in the horizon (`mass` a power of two), tries
+to take it home:
+
+- *homing* (2): if the primary line has a free or expired slot, the entry
+  moves there (`relocate`'s copy and hand-off, the source freed). The
+  lookup has just read both lines, so the attempt loads no new line;
+- *the challenge* (4): if the primary line is full, its lightest entry, if
+  it has at most half the claimant's events, is moved to its own other
+  line when that has room (one more line), and the claimant then goes
+  home.
+
+E6's homing tried on every hit found in the alternate line and at every
+insert (hits on full tables +18 ns from failed attempts); this tries at
+most log2 of a key's events, and never for a key seen once.
+
+**Rule (written after B8's reading, before B8b's).** Arms 2, 6 and 7
+against 0, as B8 read them. Worth timing if, for an arm:
+
+1. on the 80/20 stream at load 1.00, hits found in the alternate line are
+   at most half the base's share (0.0994);
+2. on the churn stream at load 1.00 the share is under the base's;
+3. the moves it adds (attempts that move an entry) are at most 5% of hits
+   on both streams at load 1.00;
+4. at load 1.00 the answers' digest equals the base's; at load 1.15
+   evictions are at most 1.10 of the base's and no key is missed;
+5. `go test -short` passes with the arm on for every filter the tests
+   make (`TF_DESPOT` in the environment).
+
+The arm to time is the one with the fewest bits that holds 1–5.
+
+## B9b The chick, with the hot table's bytes
+
+Designed after B9's reading (clause 3 failed: at load 1.15 on S1 the chick
