+## Postulates, not run
+
+**B10 Arousal: a reclaimer with a deadline.** Paper §8.3: if every line is
+provably visited once per horizon, timestamps shrink to 32 bits and 14
+bits a slot come free (E48: a 50-bit register, velocity error 0.0779 →
+0.0637); the caveat is an idle filter, where a reclaimer paced by calls
+stops. A hibernating ground squirrel does not groom, and it does not need
+a keeper to wake it either: it rouses on its own timer, does the upkeep,
+and goes back. The reclaimer needs no ticker, only a deadline: aliasing
+needs a line unvisited for half the timestamp's range (2^31 ms, 24.8
+days), so a call that finds the pass older than a quarter of the range
+sweeps 64 lines instead of 4, and one that finds it older than three
+eighths finishes the pass before it answers. An idle filter pays nothing;
+its first call after 9 idle days pays one pass (a few ms per million
+lines, the overflow table's sweep stall, E36). After an idle gap longer
+than a horizon every entry is dead, so that pass can be a clear. Reading
+to take: a clock driven through idle gaps of every length, the oldest
+unvisited line never past the bound; the largest call.
+
+**B11 Germ-line reset: a snapshot that rebases.** A restored clock
+continues the old timeline, so a lineage of snapshots ages toward
+`MaxTick` (2.2 years at 1 µs) whatever its processes' uptime, and "there
+is no rebase" (paper §8.1). The soma ages; the germ line resets, and the
+offspring starts at zero. A restore can subtract a base from every
+timestamp it reads: the base a multiple of two horizons (48 units), so the
+registers' 4-bit epochs (unit mod 16) and the overflow table's 2-bit
+slices (Horizon/2 mod 4) decode as before, and entries older than the
+base's horizon are dropped as the sweep would drop them. Reading to take:
+the answers' digest before the snapshot and after a rebasing restore,
+equal; the restored clock under three horizons.
