+604,049 keys: 0.6 hits a key. Most hits are a key's first or second
+repeat, and those find the key where its first touch put it. Homing has
+little to work with: of 40,737 tries, 14,028 found a free slot at home
+(34%), since a primary line that was full when the key arrived is mostly
+still full. A stream skewed enough for homing to matter (a few keys with
+thousands of hits) is one whose hot keys' lines are in the cache, where a
+second line costs nothing; E6 read no hit gain on a cached table. The
+second line is paid for on a DRAM miss, DRAM misses fall on keys touched
+rarely, and a key touched rarely has no history to rank it by. The
+despot's information arrives when it is no longer needed. What decides a
+cold key's first repeat is its first placement, and putting it at home
+when its primary line is full costs an insert a move (a miss) to save a
+read a miss: no gain.
